Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action gothic period drama, naturalistic faces, candlelit interiors, and period fabric.

FADE IN:

INT. OLD HOUSE HALL - NIGHT

A narrow, decaying hall lies under deep midnight shadow. A dark-clad NARRATOR stands motionless beside a closed bedroom door, carrying a tin lantern with its shutter sealed.

(SOUND: wind presses against the shuttered house; a distant clock ticks)

NARRATOR V.O.
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad?

NARRATOR V.O.
The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute.

NARRATOR V.O.
Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

INT. MR. VALE'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

MR. VALE, a frail elderly man with thin white hair and a lined face, sleeps beneath a heavy bedcover. One pale blue eye is hidden behind its closed lid.

(SOUND: the old house settles; a wall clock marks midnight)

NARRATOR V.O.
I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult.

NARRATOR V.O.
For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye! yes, it was this!

A thin slit opens in the door. The NARRATOR eases the dark lantern through, then slowly brings in his head.

(SOUND: the latch gives a faint metallic click)

NARRATOR V.O.
He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it.

NARRATOR V.O.
Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so I made up my mind to take the life of the old man.

The lantern remains closed. MR. VALE sleeps on, unaware.

NARRATOR V.O.
Every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently!

INT. OLD HOUSE HALL - NIGHT

The NARRATOR, black coat buttoned to the throat and lantern in hand, repeats the ritual at MR. VALE'S door. Night after night passes in brief, identical motions.

(SOUND: seven separate midnight clock chimes blend into one uneasy pattern)

NARRATOR V.O.
This I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed.

NARRATOR V.O.
It was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. MR. VALE'S BEDCHAMBER - MORNING

Cold daylight fills the room. MR. VALE sits upright in bed, his pale blue eye clouded by its film. The NARRATOR enters openly, composed and smiling.

(SOUND: morning birds chirp faintly beyond the closed shutters)

NARRATOR
How have you passed the night?

MR. VALE
Well enough.

The NARRATOR pours water with a steady hand. MR. VALE watches him without suspicion.

NARRATOR V.O.
I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him.

NARRATOR V.O.
He would have been a very profound old man, indeed, to suspect that every night, just at twelve, I looked in upon him while he slept.

INT. OLD HOUSE HALL - NIGHT

The NARRATOR returns on the eighth night, lantern clutched close. He turns the latch more slowly than a watch's minute hand moves.

(SOUND: rain taps the shutters; the latch barely scrapes)

NARRATOR V.O.
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door.

NARRATOR V.O.
Never before that night had I felt the extent of my own powers—of my sagacity.

The NARRATOR's mouth twitches in a suppressed chuckle.

(SOUND: a soft, involuntary chuckle)

Inside the room, MR. VALE jolts awake.

INT. MR. VALE'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

MR. VALE sits bolt upright in darkness, his white hair disordered against the pillows. The door stands open only a hand's breadth.

(SOUND: bed ropes creak; rain fades into a heavy hush)

MR. VALE
Who's there?

The NARRATOR stays hidden behind the door. The lantern shutter remains closed.

NARRATOR V.O.
I kept quite still and said nothing.

NARRATOR V.O.
For a whole hour I did not move a muscle, and in the meantime I did not hear him lie down.

MR. VALE listens into the black room. His fingers knot in the bedcover.

(SOUND: a deathwatch beetle clicks within the wall)

NARRATOR V.O.
Presently I heard a slight groan, and I knew it was the groan of mortal terror.

MR. VALE
It is nothing but the wind in the chimney.

A pause. His eye searches the darkness.

MR. VALE
It is only a mouse crossing the floor.

NARRATOR V.O.
All in vain; because Death, in approaching him, had stalked with his black shadow before him.

INT. MR. VALE'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The NARRATOR opens the lantern a fraction. A single dim ray, thin as a spider's thread, strikes MR. VALE'S open eye.

(SOUND: the lantern hinge creaks)

MR. VALE'S pale blue eye stares wide, filmed and unblinking. The NARRATOR freezes behind the door.

NARRATOR V.O.
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it.

NARRATOR V.O.
I saw it with perfect distinctness—all a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it that chilled the marrow in my bones.

A low, muffled heartbeat begins beneath the silence.

(SOUND: a quick, cotton-muffled heartbeat)

NARRATOR V.O.
There came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

NARRATOR V.O.
I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man's heart.

The NARRATOR grips the lantern until his knuckles whiten. MR. VALE gasps beneath the bedcover.

(SOUND: the heartbeat grows quicker and louder)

NARRATOR V.O.
The beating grew louder, louder! I thought the heart must burst.

NARRATOR V.O.
A new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour!

The NARRATOR throws open the lantern and lunges into the room.

(SOUND: a loud yell; MR. VALE gives one sharp shriek)

The NARRATOR drags MR. VALE from the bed and pulls the heavy bed over him.

(SOUND: wood slams against the floor; the heartbeat continues, muffled beneath the bed)

NARRATOR V.O.
For many minutes, the heart beat on with a muffled sound.

The NARRATOR kneels beside the still bed. The heartbeat finally stops.

(SOUND: the muffled heartbeat ceases; rain resumes against the shutters)

NARRATOR V.O.
At length it ceased. The old man was dead.

The NARRATOR lifts the bed, places a hand over MR. VALE'S chest, and waits.

NARRATOR V.O.
There was no pulsation. He was stone, stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. MR. VALE'S BEDCHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

By lantern light, the NARRATOR works in silence. A tub sits beneath the bed. He removes three floorboards and lowers wrapped remains between the scantlings.

(SOUND: a hand saw rasps; floorboards creak under careful pressure)

NARRATOR V.O.
First of all I dismembered the corpse. I cut off the head and the arms and the legs.

The NARRATOR replaces the planks, aligns their seams, and brushes away every trace.

(SOUND: boards settle into place; the distant clock begins to strike four)

NARRATOR V.O.
I replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye—not even his—could have detected any thing wrong.

NARRATOR V.O.
There was nothing to wash out—no stain of any kind—no blood-spot whatever.

The last bell stroke fades. A knock sounds from below.

(SOUND: firm knocking at the street door)

INT. OLD HOUSE HALL - PRE-DAWN

The NARRATOR descends the narrow stairs, lantern now burning openly. He opens the street door to reveal OFFICER REYNOLDS, OFFICER HART, and OFFICER BELL, three soberly dressed policemen.

(SOUND: rainwater drips from the officers' coats; distant horses pass on wet cobbles)

NARRATOR V.O.
There entered three men, who introduced themselves, with perfect suavity, as officers of the police.

OFFICER REYNOLDS
A shriek was heard by a neighbour during the night. We have been deputed to search the premises.

The NARRATOR smiles and gestures them inside.

NARRATOR
The shriek was my own in a dream.

NARRATOR
The old man is absent in the country.

INT. MR. VALE'S BEDCHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

The officers inspect the undisturbed room. MR. VALE'S treasures sit secure in their cabinet. The NARRATOR draws chairs close and places his own chair directly above the replaced planks.

(SOUND: chair legs scrape across the floorboards; rain softens beyond the shutters)

NARRATOR V.O.
I bade them search—search well.

NARRATOR V.O.
In the enthusiasm of my confidence, I placed my own seat upon the very spot beneath which reposed the corpse.

OFFICER HART examines the cabinet, then closes it.

OFFICER HART
Everything appears secure.

The officers sit. The NARRATOR sits over the hidden body and speaks brightly.

NARRATOR V.O.
The officers were satisfied. My manner had convinced them. I was singularly at ease.

INT. MR. VALE'S BEDCHAMBER - DAWN

Gray daylight reaches the floorboards. The officers remain seated, chatting quietly. The NARRATOR'S smile drains; sweat beads at his temple.

(SOUND: a faint ringing tone emerges beneath the officers' low conversation)

NARRATOR V.O.
Ere long, I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone.

NARRATOR V.O.
My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears: but still they sat and still chatted.

The ringing sharpens into the familiar pulse.

(SOUND: a low, dull, quick heartbeat beneath the floor)

The NARRATOR talks faster, fingers clawing the chair arms.

NARRATOR V.O.
I found that the noise was not within my ears.

OFFICER BELL smiles politely at him. The NARRATOR rises and paces over the planks.

(SOUND: heavy footsteps strike the floor; the heartbeat grows louder)

NARRATOR V.O.
I talked more fluently, and with a heightened voice. Yet the sound increased.

The NARRATOR seizes his chair and grates it across the boards.

(SOUND: chair legs grate violently; the heartbeat pounds over the scrape)

NARRATOR V.O.
Why would they not be gone?

The officers continue their quiet conversation. The NARRATOR stares at their smiling faces.

NARRATOR V.O.
They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror!

The heartbeat becomes unbearable.

(SOUND: the heartbeat hammers, loud and relentless)

NARRATOR
Villains!

The officers stand.

NARRATOR
Dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!

The NARRATOR points wildly at the floor beneath his feet.

NARRATOR
It is the beating of his hideous heart!

(SOUND: the heartbeat cuts off into absolute silence)

FADE OUT

THE END
